---@class azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplateListResult
---@field value azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplateListResultValueList The ScenarioTemplate items on this page
---@field nextLink? string The link to the next page of items

---The ScenarioTemplate items on this page
---@alias azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplateListResultValueList azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplate[]

---Model that represents the properties of a scenario template. These are the properties of the
---template's newest published version, together with the version number that identifies it.
---@class azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplateProperties
---@field latestVersion string The newest published version of the template, for example 1.2. This is the version whose content the remaining properties describe, and the version a scenario records when it is created from the template without naming one.
---@field displayName? string Localized display name of the template.
---@field description? string Description of what the template does, and what resilience question it answers.
---@field parameters? azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplatePropertiesParametersList Parameter definitions the template accepts. Values are supplied when the scenario created from this template is configured, and are referenced from an action through the `%%\{parameters.\<name\>\}%%` macro.
---@field actions azure.chaos.ScenarioTemplatePropertiesActionsList The actions that define the template's orchestration. These are the same model a scenario publishes, and carry unresolved `%%\{...\}%%` macros; the macros are substituted when the scenario runs, not when it is read or created. Three macro namespaces may appear: - `%%\{parameters.\<name\>\}%%` — resolves from the parameter values supplied when the scenario is configured. The accepted names are declared by `parameters`. - `%%\{resourceTargeting.include.\<dimension\>\}%%` — resolves from the resource targeting of the scenario's configuration (for example `zones`, `locations`). The template does not declare these; they are dimensions of the configuration supplied at run time. - `%%\{filters.\<dimension\>\}%%` — deprecated spelling of `resourceTargeting.include.`, retained only so that superseded template versions keep resolving. Do not depend on it; the newest version of every template uses `resourceTargeting.include.`. An action's `failureBehavior` overrides the template-wide `failureBehavior` for that action alone; an action that does not carry one inherits the template-wide value.
---@field failureBehavior? azure.chaos.FailureBehavior Template-wide default for how a scenario run reacts when one of this template's actions fails. An action that does not carry its own `failureBehavior` inherits this value; when neither is present the effective behavior is `Stop`. A scenario created from this template takes this policy with the rest of the template's structure, so it is also the scenario's resolved `failureBehavior`. Template versions published before this policy existed do not carry one and report it as absent, which the run resolves as `Stop`.

